Summary
The Python SDK accepts malformed initialize results that violate the shared SDK runtime protocol.
Checked commit: 47f943859bef60e4160492346772ded9b24f765a.
Expected behavior
initialize.result.serverInfo must contain both name and version as strings. The TypeScript protocol declares both fields as required, and the TypeScript SDK rejects a malformed response with SdkProtocolError.
References:

Protocol type: 


      deepseek-harness/packages/sdk/protocol/src/types.ts


        Lines 27 to 31
      in
      47f9438







           /** Wire-stable server identity returned by initialization. */ 




           export interface InitializeResult { 




             /** Wire-stable server identity (`deepseek-harness-sdk-runtime`) and version. */ 




             serverInfo: { name: string; version: string } 




           } 





TypeScript runtime validation: 


      deepseek-harness/packages/sdk/client/src/client.ts


        Lines 268 to 275
      in
      47f9438







           async initialize(params: InitializeParams): Promise<InitializeResult> { 




             const result = await this.request('initialize', { ...params }) 




             if (!isRecord(result) || !isRecord(result.serverInfo) 




               || typeof result.serverInfo.name !== 'string' || typeof result.serverInfo.version !== 'string') { 




               throw new SdkProtocolError(`initialize returned no server identity: ${JSON.stringify(result)}`) 




             } 




             return { serverInfo: { name: result.serverInfo.name, version: result.serverInfo.version } } 




           } 






Actual behavior
The Python model makes serverInfo, name, and version optional:



      deepseek-harness/python/sdk/src/deepseek_harness/models.py


        Lines 26 to 32
      in
      47f9438







           class ServerInfo(BaseModel): 




               name: str | None = None 




               version: str | None = None 














           class InitializeResponse(BaseModel): 




               serverInfo: ServerInfo | None = None 





Consequently, HarnessClient.initialize() accepts malformed runtime responses and returns successfully:



      deepseek-harness/python/sdk/src/deepseek_harness/client.py


        Lines 117 to 135
      in
      47f9438







           def initialize( 




               self, 




               *, 




               cwd: str, 




               provider: str, 




               model: str, 




               max_tokens: int | None = None, 




           ) -> InitializeResponse: 




               payload: JsonObject = { 




                   "cwd": str(Path(cwd).resolve()), 




                   "provider": provider, 




                   "model": model, 




               } 




               if max_tokens is not None: 




                   payload["maxTokens"] = max_tokens 




               try: 




                   return self.request("initialize", payload, response_model=InitializeResponse) 




               except BaseException: 




                   self.close() 





Minimal reproduction
from deepseek_harness.models import InitializeResponse

for payload in (
    {},
    {"serverInfo": {}},
    {"serverInfo": {"name": "fake"}},
):
    print(InitializeResponse.model_validate(payload).model_dump())
All three payloads are accepted. At the transport level, the same result objects returned by a fake JSON-RPC runtime also make HarnessClient.initialize() return normally.
The corresponding TypeScript client rejects these shapes because serverInfo.name and serverInfo.version are not strings.
Impact
A Python integration can mark an incompatible or malformed runtime as initialized, delaying the protocol error until a later request and producing behavior inconsistent with the TypeScript SDK.
Suggested fix
Make InitializeResponse.serverInfo, ServerInfo.name, and ServerInfo.version required strings, or add an equivalent explicit validation guard in HarnessClient.initialize(). Please also add regression tests for missing serverInfo, an empty identity object, and a missing/non-string version.
Would you consider this a bug in the Python SDK, and if so, would you prefer tightening the Pydantic model or adding an explicit guard at the client boundary?

Summary
The Python SDK can leave notification consumers blocked forever when a subscription is explicitly closed, and it accepts new subscriptions after the client has terminated even though those subscriptions have no producer.
Checked commit: 47f943859bef60e4160492346772ded9b24f765a.
Expected behavior
Closing a NotificationSubscription should stop delivery, drop queued notifications, and reject any pending or future next() call with a terminal transport/subscription error. A subscription created after the owning client has terminated should be born failed rather than waiting forever.
The TypeScript SDK, documented as the Python SDK's design twin, implements and tests this behavior:

Subscription contract/implementation: 


      deepseek-harness/packages/sdk/client/src/client.ts


        Lines 74 to 141
      in
      47f9438







           /** One client-side notification stream returned by {@link HarnessClient.subscribe}. */ 




           export interface NotificationSubscription extends AsyncIterable<HarnessNotification> { 




             /** 




              * Await the next matching notification. 




              * @returns the notification; after the runtime died, drains what was 




              * already delivered and then rejects; after {@link close}, rejects 




              * immediately (the queue is dropped). 




              */ 




             next(): Promise<HarnessNotification> 









             /** 




              * Drain one already-delivered notification without waiting. 




              * @returns the next queued notification, or `undefined` when none is queued. 




              */ 




             tryNext(): HarnessNotification | undefined 









             /** Detach from the client; queued items drop and pending waiters reject. */ 




             close(): void 




           } 









           /** Internal producer side of a public notification subscription. */ 




           class NotificationSubscriptionImpl implements NotificationSubscription { 




             constructor( 




               private readonly state: SubscriptionState, 




               private readonly unsubscribe: () => void, 




             ) {} 









             /** 




              * Await the next matching notification. 




              * @returns the notification; after the runtime died, drains what was 




              * already delivered and then rejects; after {@link close}, rejects 




              * immediately (the queue is dropped). 




              */ 




             next(): Promise<HarnessNotification> { 




               const queued = this.state.queue.shift() 




               if (queued !== undefined) return Promise.resolve(queued) 




               if (this.state.failure !== undefined) return Promise.reject(this.state.failure) 




               return new Promise((resolve, reject) => { 




                 this.state.waiters.push({ resolve, reject }) 




               }) 




             } 









             /** 




              * Drain one already-delivered notification without waiting. 




              * @returns the next queued notification, or `undefined` when none is queued. 




              */ 




             tryNext(): HarnessNotification | undefined { 




               return this.state.queue.shift() 




             } 









             /** Detach from the client; queued items drop and pending waiters reject. */ 




             close(): void { 




               this.unsubscribe() 




               // The drop is part of this method's contract; a runtime-death fail() keeps 




               // the queue so already-delivered notifications remain drainable. 




               this.state.queue.length = 0 




               this.fail(new TransportClosedError('notification subscription closed')) 




             } 









             /** 




              * Reject pending and future waits (delivery stops; the first failure wins). 




              * Already-queued notifications remain drainable via {@link next}/{@link tryNext}. 




              * @param error - the terminal failure delivered to waiters. 




              */ 




             fail(error: Error): void { 




               this.state.failure ??= error 




               for (const waiter of this.state.waiters.splice(0)) waiter.reject(this.state.failure) 




             } 





Post-termination admission: 


      deepseek-harness/packages/sdk/client/src/client.ts


        Lines 335 to 351
      in
      47f9438







             /** 




              * Subscribe to server notifications. 




              * @param filter - optional predicate; omitted means every notification. 




              * @returns the subscription handle; close it to stop delivery. After 




              * {@link close} or runtime death the handle is born failed — there is no 




              * producer left, so `next()` rejects instead of waiting forever. 




              */ 




             subscribe(filter?: NotificationFilter): NotificationSubscription { 




               const id = String(this.subscriptionSerial++) 




               const state: SubscriptionState = { queue: [], waiters: [], filter, failure: undefined } 




               const subscription = new NotificationSubscriptionImpl(state, () => { this.subscriptions.delete(id) }) 




               if (this.closeTask !== undefined || this.exitCode !== undefined || this.spawnError !== undefined) { 




                 subscription.fail(this.closedError('DeepSeek Harness runtime closed')) 




                 return subscription 




               } 




               this.subscriptions.set(id, subscription) 




               return subscription 





Regression tests: 


      deepseek-harness/packages/sdk/client/tests/sdk-client.spec.ts


        Lines 407 to 444
      in
      47f9438







           it('close() drops queued notifications; runtime death keeps them drainable', async () => { 




             const client = new HarnessClient(fakeLaunch()) 




             await client.initialize({ cwd: process.cwd(), provider: 'p', model: 'm' }) 




             const closed = client.subscribe() 




             const drainable = client.subscribe() 




             await client.prompt('queue-drop', normalizeInput('go')) 




             expect(closed.tryNext()).toBeDefined() 




             closed.close() 




             // Manual close drops the rest of the queue outright. 




             expect(closed.tryNext()).toBeUndefined() 




             await expect(closed.next()).rejects.toThrow('notification subscription closed') 




             // Runtime teardown, by contrast, only stops FUTURE delivery: what was 




             // already delivered before close() stays drainable. 




             await client.close() 




             expect(drainable.tryNext()).toBeDefined() 




           }) 









           it('subscriptions created after termination are born failed', async () => { 




             const client = new HarnessClient(fakeLaunch()) 




             await client.initialize({ cwd: process.cwd(), provider: 'p', model: 'm' }) 




             await client.close() 




             // No producer can ever feed this subscription; next() must not park forever. 




             await expect(client.subscribe().next()).rejects.toThrow(TransportClosedError) 









             const dead = new HarnessClient(fakeLaunch({ FAKE_EXIT_BEFORE_INIT: '1' })) 




             cleanups.push(() => dead.close()) 




             await dead.initialize({ cwd: process.cwd(), provider: 'p', model: 'm' }).catch(() => {}) 




             await expect(dead.subscribe().next()).rejects.toThrow(TransportClosedError) 




           }) 









           it('closes subscriptions with the runtime and rejects parked waiters', async () => { 




             const client = new HarnessClient(fakeLaunch()) 




             await client.initialize({ cwd: process.cwd(), provider: 'p', model: 'm' }) 




             const subscription = client.subscribe() 




             const parked = subscription.next() 




             await client.close() 




             await expect(parked).rejects.toThrow(TransportClosedError) 




           }) 






Actual behavior
In the Python SDK, NotificationSubscription.close() only unregisters the queue, while next() unconditionally blocks on queue.get() and does not check _closed:



      deepseek-harness/python/sdk/src/deepseek_harness/client.py


        Lines 507 to 545
      in
      47f9438







           class NotificationSubscription: 




               def __init__( 




                   self, 




                   client: HarnessClient, 




                   subscription_id: str, 




                   notifications: queue.Queue[Notification | BaseException], 




               ) -> None: 




                   self._client = client 




                   self._subscription_id = subscription_id 




                   self._notifications = notifications 




                   self._closed = False 









               def __enter__(self) -> "NotificationSubscription": 




                   return self 









               def __exit__(self, _exc_type, _exc, _tb) -> None: 




                   self.close() 









               def close(self) -> None: 




                   if self._closed: 




                       return 




                   self._closed = True 




                   self._client._unsubscribe_notifications(self._subscription_id) 









               def next(self) -> Notification: 




                   item = self._notifications.get() 




                   if isinstance(item, BaseException): 




                       raise item 




                   return item 









               def drain(self, on_notification: Callable[[Notification], None]) -> None: 




                   while True: 




                       try: 




                           item = self._notifications.get_nowait() 




                       except queue.Empty: 




                           return 




                       if isinstance(item, BaseException): 




                           raise item 




                       on_notification(item) 





Also, subscribe_notifications() registers a new queue without checking whether the client has already terminated:



      deepseek-harness/python/sdk/src/deepseek_harness/client.py


        Lines 192 to 200
      in
      47f9438







           def subscribe_notifications( 




               self, 




               notification_filter: NotificationFilter | None = None, 




           ) -> "NotificationSubscription": 




               subscription_id = str(uuid.uuid4()) 




               notifications: queue.Queue[Notification | BaseException] = queue.Queue() 




               with self._lock: 




                   self._notification_subscribers[subscription_id] = (notifications, notification_filter) 




               return NotificationSubscription(self, subscription_id, notifications) 





The existing Python client-close path does signal subscriptions that were registered before termination; the issue is specifically explicit handle close and post-termination subscription admission.
Minimal reproduction
import threading
import time

from deepseek_harness.client import HarnessClient

client = HarnessClient()
subscription = client.subscribe_notifications()
returned = threading.Event()

def waiter():
    subscription.next()
    returned.set()

thread = threading.Thread(target=waiter, daemon=True)
thread.start()
time.sleep(0.05)
subscription.close()
thread.join(0.2)

assert thread.is_alive()  # remains blocked after close
The same behavior was reproduced with a fake stdio runtime for a subscription created after client.close(): its next() call remained parked after 200 ms. The reproduction also showed that an already-registered subscription does terminate on client close.
Impact
Low-level Python consumers can leak blocked worker threads during explicit subscription cleanup. Code that creates a subscription after client shutdown can wait indefinitely instead of receiving a fail-fast transport error. This can make cleanup or higher-level notification loops hang without a bounded timeout.
Suggested fix
Give each subscription a terminal failure path: on explicit close, clear queued items and wake pending waiters with TransportClosedError; make future next() calls reject when _closed; and track terminal client state so post-close subscriptions are created already failed. Please add regression tests for explicit close, queued-item dropping, post-client-close subscription admission, and sibling subscription isolation.
Would you consider this a bug in the Python SDK, and if so, would you prefer a queue sentinel/error path or a shared subscription state object similar to the TypeScript implementation?

描述
在 Windows 上使用 edit 工具(或 writeText)覆盖一个已存在的文件时,经常直接失败,报错形如:
Error: ReplaceFileW EIO (Win32 32): E:\vibecoding\xxx.md


新建文件正常,只有覆盖已有文件时出现;
频率高,但并非 100%;对同一文件立即重试通常可成功——指向瞬时文件锁,而非确定性缺陷。

环境

OS: Windows 11 25H2(build 26200.8875),64 位
DSH: 0.1.0-rc.6(npm 全局安装)
安全软件:仅 Windows Defender 实时防护(RealTimeProtectionEnabled=True);无第三方杀软
磁盘:本地 NTFS 固定盘(非网络盘、非 OneDrive 目录)

根因分析
1. 错误串来源与含义
dsh-fs-local 的 win32Error()(src/fsio.ts,安装包中为 lib/index.js 34–51 行)只把 Win32 2/3 映射为 ENOENT、5 映射为 EACCES,其余所有错误码一律映射为 EIO。因此消息中的 "EIO" 只是兜底标签,真实错误码是 Win32 32 = ERROR_SHARING_VIOLATION("文件正被另一个进程使用,且未授予 FILE_SHARE_DELETE")。
2. 调用链
edit/write 覆盖已有文件时,writeFileAtomic(lib/index.js 461 行起)执行:

在目标旁创建 .文件名.<pid>.<uuid>.tmpdir/ staging 目录;
open("wx", 0o600) 写入临时文件 → fsync → close(483–497 行);
立即调用 ReplaceFileW(目标, 临时文件, NULL)(83–86 行,经 koffi 直调 kernel32)——为保留目标文件的 DACL(见设计笔记 2026-07-19-windows-atomic-write-dacl-preservation);
失败处理:只有 ENOENT 才回退 rename(504–509 行),其余错误(包括 32)直接抛出,无任何重试。

3. 为什么失败
ReplaceFileW 要求目标文件与替换文件在调用瞬间不被任何进程以不带 FILE_SHARE_DELETE 的方式打开。Node/libuv 打开文件时带 FILE_SHARE_READ|WRITE|DELETE,DSH 自身句柄不会造成此问题。最可疑的占用者是 Windows Defender 实时防护对刚创建的 staging 临时文件的扫描句柄——DSH 在 close 后毫秒级调用 ReplaceFileW,扫描尚未结束 → ERROR_SHARING_VIOLATION。这解释了"频率高、非 100%、重试即过"的特征。
复现与实测
瞬时锁,无法稳定复现。实测记录(Windows 11 25H2 / build 26200.8875):



实验
结果




按 DSH 精确调用形态(node + koffi + toNamespacedPath + staging 子目录 + wx/fsync/close)连续压测 20 次
20/20 成功


DSH 服务进程内用 edit 工具真实编辑工作区文件
3/3 成功


MoveFileExW(MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING)(即 fs.rename)对照组
始终成功



失败概率与 Defender 扫描时长相关(签名刚更新、目录冷启动时窗口更宽)。
最小压测脚本(与 DSH 相同调用形态):
// 依赖 DSH 的 koffi;签名与 replaceFileWin32 一致
const koffi = require('koffi');
const kernel32 = koffi.load('kernel32.dll');
const replaceFileW = kernel32.func(
  'int __stdcall ReplaceFileW(const char16_t *replaced, const char16_t *replacement, ' +
  'const char16_t *backup, uint32_t flags, void *exclude, void *reserved)');

// 1. 在目标同卷目录创建 staging 临时文件(如 .name.tmpdir/name.tmp)
// 2. 写入内容、fsync、close
// 3. 立即调用:
const r = replaceFileW(toNamespacedPath(target), toNamespacedPath(temp), null, 0, null, null);
// r === 0 时 GetLastError() 通常为 32(ERROR_SHARING_VIOLATION)
预期行为
瞬时文件锁(杀软扫描、索引器等)不应导致编辑失败:

应先做短退避重试(几十毫秒级);
重试仍失败时,回退到 MoveFileExW(MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING)(即 fs.rename)——临时文件在写入前已拷贝目标 DACL(lib/index.js 485 行),rename 不会丢失 ACL,仅失去 ReplaceFileW 的元数据保留语义,作为兜底可接受。

建议修复

对 ReplaceFileW 失败(至少 ERROR_SHARING_VIOLATION=32)增加短退避重试(如 5 次 × 20–50ms)后再报错;
完善错误码映射:32 → EACCES/EBUSY,避免误导性的 EIO(2/3→ENOENT、5→EACCES 之外不应全部兜底为 EIO);
重试仍失败时回退 fs.rename(temp, target) 作为兜底(本机实测 MoveFileExW 始终可用);
在 dsh-fs-local README 的 Known Limitations 中补充 Windows 瞬时共享冲突的说明。

同类问题的既有修法(供参考)

esengine/DeepSeek-Reasonix #4193: fix(desktop): retry atomic writes past transient Windows locks
zed-industries/zed #30222: fs: Fall back from atomic write to regular fs write when file handle is in use on Windows
LLVM: Improve reliability of file renaming in Windows

用户侧缓解(非本 issue 修复范围)

将工作区加入 Windows Defender 排除项(可根治本机问题);
失败时立即重试编辑;
若某个固定文件反复失败,用 Sysinternals handle.exe 排查占用进程。

{
"code": "const { stdout } = await tools.bash({ command: "pwd && ls -la", description: "Check current directory" });\nreturn stdout;",
"description": "Check current working directory"
}
OUT
Error: unknown tool ""

一句话说明
模型输出被 max-tokens 截断且带工具调用时，保存的 assistant 消息与 replay state 块数不一致，下一轮请求直接报 invalid pi-ai replay state: block count does not match assistant content，整轮失败。

复现步骤

通过 dsh-llm-pi-ai 适配器（如 openai-completions 路由 + compat.thinkingFormat: deepseek）调用模型，让它输出 reasoning + text + tool-call，并让回答在 maxTokens 处被截断（工具调用尚未完成）；
流以 finish { reason: { kind: "max-tokens" }, replayState } 结束，其中 replayState.blocks 含 3 块 [reasoning, text, tool-call]；
循环把 assistant 消息内容存为 2 块 [reasoning, text]（BlockAssembler.blocks() 在 max-tokens 时会丢弃 tool-call 块），但 message.source.replayState 仍保留完整 3 块；
用户继续会话（如输入“继续”），下一轮请求构建时 replayedAssistant() 校验 state.blocks.length !== message.content.length（3 !== 2），抛出 INVALID_REPLAY_STATE，整轮失败。

实际结果 vs 预期结果

实际：下一轮直接报 invalid pi-ai replay state: block count does not match assistant content，本轮运行失败；只能新建会话或 fork 到失败消息之前恢复。
预期：被截断的消息要么同步其 replay state，要么丢弃 replay state，使后续请求正常进行。

根因

BlockAssembler.blocks()（dsh-llm）：finish.kind === 'max-tokens' 时丢弃 tool-call 块（“tools that cannot be executed safely”）。
适配器 dsh-llm-pi-ai 的 done 事件：replayState: toPiReplayState(event.message) 基于 pi-ai 完整 message.content 投影（3 块），不知道 assembler 侧丢弃了块。

因此保存的 source.replayState 描述 3 块，而持久化内容只有 2 块，replayedAssistant() 在下一轮请求时正确地拒绝了这个不匹配。
建议修复

在 BlockAssembler 因截断丢弃 tool-call 块时，同步丢弃 replayState 中对应条目（或 finish.kind === 'max-tokens' 且发生丢弃时直接清空 replay state）；和/或
在 dsh-llm-pi-ai 中对 length 停止不携带 replay state，或按循环将要保存的同一截断内容投影 replayState.blocks。

截断步骤丢弃 replay state 最安全：截断后的消息正是 README 所述“内容被改写必须丢弃 replay state”的情形，丢失一次 provider 元数据无碍。
最小复现
import { BlockAssembler } from "@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm";
const a = new BlockAssembler();
a.push({ type: "block-start", index: 0, blockType: "text" });
a.push({ type: "text-delta", index: 0, text: "hi" });
a.push({ type: "block-end", index: 0, block: { type: "text", text: "hi" } });
a.push({ type: "block-start", index: 1, blockType: "tool-call" });
a.push({ type: "tool-call-delta", index: 1, id: "c1", name: "f", argumentsDelta: "{}" });
a.push({ type: "block-end", index: 1, block: { type: "tool-call", id: "c1", name: "f", arguments: "{}" } });
a.push({ type: "finish", reason: { kind: "max-tokens" }, replayState: {
  kind: "pi-ai", version: 1, api: "openai-completions", provider: "p", model: "m",
  stopReason: "length", blocks: [{ type: "text" }, { type: "tool-call" }],
} });
// a.blocks() === [{type:"text"}] 但 a.replayState.blocks.length === 2 → 不匹配
环境

@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm-pi-ai 0.1.0-rc.6（master 同样存在）
@deepseek-ai/dsh-llm 0.1.0-rc.6
@earendil-works/pi-ai 0.82.1
路由：openai-completions，compat.thinkingFormat: deepseek，defaultMaxTokens 已配置；模型 deepseek-v4-flash-0731

自定义模型，发送你好，然后返回失败：
本轮运行失败400: {"message":"The request is invalid: Invalid schema for function 'get_goal': null is not of type "array". Please check the request body, required fields, and request format. (request id: 2026081417320237172231978633089)","type":"invalid_request_error","param":"","code":null}
源码安装的，它自我排查问题时，总是终端，类似上图中情况

版本 / Version

@deepseek-ai/dsh 0.1.0-rc.6（npm 全局安装）
复现环境：macOS，Web GUI 打开会话历史

现象 / Symptom
某个会话的历史无法加载，接口返回：
history unavailable for session "session-…": RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded

复现 / Reproduction

在一个会话中发起一个超大工具调用——我这里是通过 cordis_define 定义含约 49KB 内嵌源码的插件，工具调用参数 JSON 约 1.7MB。
该调用被流式切成约 25.6 万个 chunk（会话日志中对应 assistant/chunk 事件）。
之后每次从 Web GUI 加载该会话历史都会触发上面的栈溢出错误（无需任何其他操作）。

根因 / Root cause
会话日志中该 assistant/message 事件的 sourceEventSeqs 数组有 255,980 个条目（引用全部被其覆盖的 chunk 序号）。dsh-host-apiproxy 的 paginate() 用展开运算符把整个数组传给 Math.min：
const groupStart = sources !== void 0 && sources.length > 0 ? Math.min(event.seq, ...sources) : event.seq;
Math.min(seq, ...255980 个参数) 超过 V8 的参数上限，直接抛出 RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded。日志本身无损坏（JSON 深度 ≤ 10，全部可解析），纯属该处调用方式问题。
修复建议 / Suggested fix
两处（lib/index.js 是实际加载的 bundle，lib/types/api-proxy.js 是其源码副本），本地已验证：
// 修复前
const groupStart = sources !== void 0 && sources.length > 0 ? Math.min(event.seq, ...sources) : event.seq;
// 修复后
let groupStart = event.seq;
if (sources !== void 0 && sources.length > 0) {
    for (const source of sources) if (source < groupStart) groupStart = source;
}
另外建议从源头缓解：单条 sourceEventSeqs 达到数十万级时，要么限制单次工具调用的大小/流式 chunk 数量，要么对大工具调用参数做压缩存储，避免再次出现同类事件。
影响 / Impact
任何包含此类超大工具调用的会话历史都无法在 GUI 中打开（agent 侧仍可运行，仅历史展示与续读失败）。遇到同样问题的朋友请给本帖点赞/upvote，方便官方按优先级处理。

Describe the bug
Resuming a session whose agent preset includes the Cordis toolset fails with:
resume failed for session "session-…": Error: agent-presets: preset "cordis" failed to mount: failed to apply loader entry tool-cordis (@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-cordis): Host Cordis inspect provider "Service" is already registered

Once this happens the conversation can no longer be reopened at all — every prompt attempt fails the same way. The session's stored data is intact (it is a mount-time failure only, not data corruption).
Environment

DSH 0.1.0-rc.6, web profile, Windows
Reproduced while running two sessions in one host process: one on the shipped cordis preset, one on a locally authored preset that also mounts a tool-cordis row

Root cause (from source)

CordisInspectRegistryService is a process-global singleton created by DynamicCordisRunnerService on the host plane (new CordisInspectRegistryService(ctx), service key cordisInspect, in @deepseek-ai/dsh-cordis-host-runner). Its register() throws Host Cordis inspect provider "<id>" is already registered when the provider id already exists in its Map.
@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-cordis registers the first-party Host inspect providers (Service, Event, Builtin, Tool) unconditionally in apply():
for (const provider of hostInspectProviders(ctx))
  ctx.effect(() => ctx.cordisInspect.register(provider), `tool-cordis: inspect ${provider.manifest.id}`);

Because the registry is a process singleton, the vanilla package can be mounted only once per process. Two sessions whose presets both carry a tool-cordis row (e.g. the shipped cordis preset and any locally authored preset that copies it) collide: whichever mounts second throws, which fails the whole preset mount, which fails the session resume. The failure is order-dependent: the first mount wins and holds the ids until its fiber is disposed.

In my case it appeared in two shapes. First, a session on the shipped cordis preset that was torn down abnormally (a command error interrupted its last turn) left its four provider ids behind in the process-global registry, so every subsequent resume of that same session collided with its own leftover registration. Second, after a host restart, resuming the same session while a locally authored preset (a patched copy of dsh-tool-cordis) was already live failed identically, because the vanilla package threw on Service. Either way the conversation stays unrecoverable until the holder goes away or the process restarts.
Workaround I use locally
I patched a copy of dsh-tool-cordis whose registration loop catches the duplicate error and skips it (the providers are identical, so sharing one registration is correct):
for (const provider of hostInspectProviders(ctx)) ctx.effect(() => {
  try {
    return ctx.cordisInspect.register(provider);
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof Error && error.message.includes(`inspect provider "${provider.manifest.id}" is already registered`)) return () => {};
    throw error;
  }
}, `tool-cordis: inspect ${provider.manifest.id}`);
This makes the patched preset tolerant when it mounts second, but the vanilla cordis preset still fails when it mounts after another tool-cordis instance — the collision is only fixed in one direction.
Suggested fixes

Make CordisInspectRegistryService.register() idempotent for duplicate ids (reference-counted, or replace-and-return-disposer).
Or register the first-party Host inspect providers once on the host plane (they are static catalogs plus live Tool scope — nothing about them is per-session) instead of from every preset mount.
Or scope the registry per agent/session.
Regardless of the fix, improve the failure surface: today the error is an opaque "resume failed" with no hint that another live session holds the provider, and the session stays stuck until that holder goes away.

