    @deepseek-ai/dsh-spill@0.1.5-rc.2 \
    @deepseek-ai/dsh-subagent-in-process-driver@0.1.5-rc.2 \
    @deepseek-ai/dsh-util-time@0.1.5-rc.2
./node_modules/.bin/dsh web
After that the server starts cleanly on http://127.0.0.1:3080 and the token-URL flow works.
Suggested fix

Move all 18 (and likely a few more) @deepseek-ai/* peer-only packages from peerDependencies to dependencies in apps/cli/package.json. They are hard runtime requirements, not "host-provided" peers. (pnpm-lock.yaml already records them as resolvable — npx just doesn't reach the transitive peers.)
Republish the dist-tags. Either (a) move every package to its current next/alpha release and re-point latest, or (b) republish 0.1.5-rc.2 across the whole tree so latest matches. Otherwise npm install <pkg>@latest will keep picking 0.0.1-rc.1.
The release workflow (scripts/release.sh or equivalent) should either pin explicit versions in apps/cli/package.json or refuse to publish if dist-tags.latest lags behind the CLI's release commit.

Notes

The README states "Submit feedback or bug reports through GitHub Discussions" — filing here per that.
A user could not have discovered this from the docs: the only documented install is npx @deepseek-ai/dsh web, which currently fails. The dev path (git clone + pnpm install) is unaffected because pnpm resolves transitives through the workspace.
Filed from a fresh macOS box; no global dsh install involved.

Thanks for the project — happy to send a PR with the dep-list patch if maintainers are open to it.

Summary
When an MCP server advertises even one tool whose inputSchema uses a JSON Schema keyword outside the enforced subset, dsh-mcp-client ends up registering zero tools from that server. The all-or-nothing swap in syncTools rolls back the whole generation because a single schema fails assertSupportedJsonSchema.
In practice this makes Playwright MCP completely unusable through DSH: every one of its 26 tool schemas carries "inputSchema": { "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema", ... }, so the entire browser server is dropped. Sessions then have no mcp__browser__* tools at all, and agents typically fall back to launching their own headless Chromium from bash — which sites such as Google risk-block — producing confusing downstream failures that look like an IP or login problem instead of a tool-registration one.
Reproduction

Install and start Playwright MCP (streamable HTTP):
npm i -g @playwright/mcp@0.0.81
playwright-mcp --cdp-endpoint http://127.0.0.1:9223 --port 8935

Add this to a profile cordis.patch.yml:
- insert:
  - id: mcp-browser
    name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client'
    config:
      serverName: browser
      transport: streamable-http
      url: http://127.0.0.1:8935/mcp

Start a new DSH session → no mcp__browser__* tools exist in the tool catalog (verified on 0.1.5-rc.2 and 0.1.6-alpha.2; other streamable-http servers in the same profile register fine).
The server itself is healthy — a direct MCP handshake works:
POST /mcp initialize → HTTP 200, mcp-session-id header,
result.serverInfo = { name: "Playwright", version: "1.64.0-alpha-2026-09-14" }
tools/list → 26 tools



Current behavior
Running assertSupportedJsonSchema (from @deepseek-ai/dsh-tools) against each of the 26 Playwright MCP tool schemas:
FAIL: browser_navigate — unsupported JSON schema: schema.$schema is not a supported keyword
     (subset: type/oneOf/properties/required/additionalProperties/items/enum/const + annotations)
... all 26 tools fail on `$schema`; additionally:
FAIL: browser_drop           — schema.properties.data.propertyNames is not a supported keyword;
                               schema.properties.data.additionalProperties must be a boolean
FAIL: browser_network_request — schema.properties.index.minimum / .maximum are not supported keywords

In packages/mcp/mcp-client/src/tools.ts, syncTools builds the full next generation in phase 1 and registers it in phase 2. The ToolRuntime registration validates parameters against the enforced subset; when one tool's schema throws, the catch block disposes everything already registered for that server:
mcp-client(browser): tool registration failed, no tools registered: ...

Result: zero tools from the server — not 25 of 26. The only trace is a single error log line; the session otherwise looks healthy.
Note the asymmetry in the same file: supportedOutputSchema() already degrades gracefully for unsupported output schemas (try/catch → undefined fallback), while an unsupported input schema takes down the whole server.
Expected behavior
One tool with an out-of-subset schema should not disable an entire MCP server. Suggested options, in order of preference:

Sanitize at the MCP boundary: strip keywords outside the enforced subset from incoming tool inputSchemas (they are advisory to the model anyway; the subset engine cannot validate them) and log a warning per affected tool. This preserves the full server and keeps registration total.
Degrade per tool: register the tools with supported schemas, skip the offenders with a logged warning — instead of the all-or-nothing rollback.
At minimum, accept $schema as an annotation (it is a pure annotation in JSON Schema 2020-12) — though this alone would not fix Playwright MCP (propertyNames, minimum/maximum, object-valued additionalProperties would still fail).

Environment

DSH 0.1.5-rc.2 (npm) and 0.1.6-alpha.2 / master ddefc45fbc — same code path in packages/mcp/mcp-client/src/tools.ts
Linux x86_64, Node v22.23.2 / v24.19.0
@playwright/mcp 0.0.81 (server "Playwright" 1.64.0-alpha-2026-09-14), streamable-http transport

Workaround (running in production)
A stdio proxy in front of the HTTP server that sanitizes tools/list schemas to the supported subset and forwards everything else verbatim. Wired into DSH as a transport: stdio entry pointing at the script below; verified end-to-end (all 26 tools registered, real browsing works). ~180 lines, pure Node, zero dependencies:

browser-bridge.mjs
#!/usr/bin/env node
/**
 * browser-bridge — stdio MCP proxy in front of a local streamable-http MCP server.
 * Strips unsupported keywords from tools/list inputSchemas (DSH enforced subset:
 * type/oneOf/properties/required/additionalProperties/items/enum/const + annotations
 * description/title/default/examples; additionalProperties coerced to boolean).
 */
const UPSTREAM = process.env.BROWSER_BRIDGE_UPSTREAM ?? 'http://127.0.0.1:8935/mcp';
const SERVER_INFO = { name: 'browser-bridge', version: '1.0.0' };

const STRUCTURAL = new Set(['type', 'oneOf', 'properties', 'required', 'additionalProperties', 'items', 'enum', 'const']);
const ANNOTATIONS = new Set(['description', 'title', 'default', 'examples']);

function sanitize(node) {
  if (Array.isArray(node)) return node.map(sanitize);
  if (node && typeof node === 'object') {
    const out = {};
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(node)) {
      if (!STRUCTURAL.has(k) && !ANNOTATIONS.has(k)) continue; // drop $schema, minimum, maximum, propertyNames, ...
      if (k === 'properties' && v && typeof v === 'object' && !Array.isArray(v)) {
        const props = {};
        for (const [pk, pv] of Object.entries(v)) props[pk] = sanitize(pv);
        out[k] = props;
      } else if (k === 'additionalProperties' && v !== undefined && typeof v !== 'boolean') {
        out[k] = true; // object-valued additionalProperties → "anything allowed"
      } else {
        out[k] = sanitize(v);
      }
    }
    return out;
  }
  return node;
}

let sessionId = null;
let initializing = null;

async function post(body, withSession = true) {
  const headers = { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Accept': 'application/json, text/event-stream' };
  if (withSession && sessionId) headers['mcp-session-id'] = sessionId;
  const res = await fetch(UPSTREAM, { method: 'POST', headers, body: JSON.stringify(body) });
  const sid = res.headers.get('mcp-session-id');
  if (sid) sessionId = sid;
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`upstream HTTP ${res.status}: ${(await res.text().catch(() => '')).slice(0, 200)}`);
  return await res.text();
}

function parseBody(text) {
  const trimmed = text.trim();
  if (trimmed === '') return [];
  if (trimmed.startsWith('{')) return [JSON.parse(trimmed)];
  const out = [];
  for (const line of trimmed.split('\n')) {
    if (line.startsWith('data:')) {
      try { out.push(JSON.parse(line.slice(5).trim())); } catch { /* keep-alive/comment */ }
    }
  }
  return out;
}

async function ensureSession() {
  if (sessionId) return;
  if (!initializing) {
    initializing = (async () => {
      sessionId = null;
      await post({ jsonrpc: '2.0', id: 1, method: 'initialize',
        params: { protocolVersion: '2025-03-26', capabilities: {}, clientInfo: SERVER_INFO } }, false);
      await post({ jsonrpc: '2.0', method: 'notifications/initialized' }).catch(() => {});
    })().finally(() => { initializing = null; });
  }
  await initializing;
}

async function upstreamCall(method, params, id) {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 2; attempt++) {
    try {
      await ensureSession();
      const text = await post({ jsonrpc: '2.0', id, method, params });
      const msgs = parseBody(text).filter((m) => m && (m.id === id || m.method));
      const resp = msgs.find((m) => m.id === id);
      if (!resp) throw new Error(`no response for id ${id} in upstream reply`);
      return resp;
    } catch (err) {
      if (attempt === 0 && /session|404|HTTP 5/i.test(String(err.message))) { sessionId = null; continue; }
      throw err;
    }
  }
}

function send(message) { process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(message) + '\n'); }
function reply(id, result) { send({ jsonrpc: '2.0', id, result }); }
function replyError(id, code, message) { send({ jsonrpc: '2.0', id, error: { code, message } }); }

async function handle(request) {
  const { id, method, params } = request;
  switch (method) {
    case 'initialize':
      reply(id, { protocolVersion: params?.protocolVersion ?? '2025-03-26', capabilities: { tools: {} }, serverInfo: SERVER_INFO });
      return;
    case 'ping':
      reply(id, {});
      return;
    case 'tools/list': {
      const resp = await upstreamCall('tools/list', {}, id ?? 1);
      if (resp.error) throw new Error(resp.error.message ?? 'upstream tools/list error');
      const tools = (resp.result?.tools ?? []).map((t) => ({ ...t, inputSchema: sanitize(t.inputSchema ?? { type: 'object' }) }));
      reply(id, { tools });
      return;
    }
    case 'tools/call': {
      let resp;
      try {
        resp = await upstreamCall('tools/call', params, id ?? 1);
      } catch (err) {
        const msg = String(err?.message ?? err);
        if (/fetch failed|ECONNREFUSED|HTTP 5\d\d/i.test(msg)) {
          reply(id, { content: [{ type: 'text', text: `Upstream MCP server not reachable: ${msg}` }], isError: true });
        } else {
          replyError(id, -32603, msg);
        }
        return;
      }
      if (resp.error) throw new Error(resp.error.message ?? 'upstream tools/call error');
      reply(id, resp.result ?? { content: [{ type: 'text', text: '(empty result)' }] });
      return;
    }
    default:
      if (id !== undefined) replyError(id, -32601, `method not found: ${String(method)}`);
  }
}

process.stdin.setEncoding('utf8');
let buffer = '';
let stdinClosed = false;
let inFlight = 0;
function maybeExit() { if (stdinClosed && inFlight === 0) process.exit(0); }
process.stdin.on('data', (chunk) => {
  buffer += chunk;
  let idx;
  while ((idx = buffer.indexOf('\n')) >= 0) {
    const line = buffer.slice(0, idx).trim();
    buffer = buffer.slice(idx + 1);
    if (line === '') continue;
    let request;
    try { request = JSON.parse(line); } catch {
      send({ jsonrpc: '2.0', id: null, error: { code: -32700, message: 'parse error' } });
      continue;
    }
    if (request && typeof request === 'object' && request.jsonrpc === '2.0' && request.method) {
      inFlight++;
      handle(request)
        .catch((err) => { if (request.id !== undefined) replyError(request.id, -32603, `internal error: ${err?.message ?? String(err)}`); })
        .finally(() => { inFlight--; maybeExit(); });
    } else if (request && request.id !== undefined) {
      replyError(request.id, -32600, 'invalid request');
    }
  }
});
process.stdin.on('close', () => { stdinClosed = true; maybeExit(); });

