Summary
If an attachment object is removed from the local attachment store
($DSH_HOME/attachments/v1/objects/<xx>/<sha256>) while a session still
references it, that session becomes permanently unusable: every subsequent
message fails with Attachment object is missing. and no recovery is possible
without manually editing the session log.
Steps to reproduce

Send an image in a session, so the session log records an image block:
{"type":"image","attachment":{"attachmentId":"sha256:<hash>","mediaType":"image/jpeg",...}}

Delete the backing file:
rm "$DSH_HOME/attachments/v1/objects/<xx>/<hash>"

Send any new message in the same session.

Actual behavior
Every turn fails:
turn/end reason = { kind: "error", error: { message: "Attachment object is missing." } }

The session cannot be recovered. Turn N failed, and turns N+1, N+2 ... all fail
the same way, because every request replays the full history including the
dangling reference.
Expected behavior
A missing attachment should degrade gracefully: skip that image (or substitute a
text placeholder) and deliver the rest of the conversation. Losing one cached
image should not make a session unusable.
Root cause
@deepseek-ai/dsh-attachment-local/lib/index.js:610 (and :746 for file
attachments) throws unconditionally on ENOENT:
if (error instanceof Error && "code" in error && error.code === "ENOENT")
    throw new AttachmentError("Attachment object is missing.", "ATTACHMENT_NOT_FOUND");
There is no degradation anywhere up the stack. Additionally ATTACHMENT_NOT_FOUND
is not in the default retryableCodes
(EMPTY_RESPONSE / RATE_LIMIT / SERVER / TIMEOUT / TRANSPORT), so the
failure is immediate and never retried — while the UI surfaces it as
DeepSeek Messages transport failed, which is misleading (it looks like a
network problem).
Suggested fix
Catch ATTACHMENT_NOT_FOUND at request-projection time and drop the unreadable
image block for that request only. Verified locally on both providers:
dsh-llm-deepseek — prepareImages (~L1417)
for (const message of messages) for (const ref of imageRefs(message.content)) {
    if (versions.has(ref.attachmentId)) continue;
    try {
        versions.set(ref.attachmentId, await attachments.readImageRequest(ref, resolveRequestImageTarget(model, ref), signal));
    } catch (error) {
        signal?.throwIfAborted();
        if (error?.code !== "ATTACHMENT_NOT_FOUND") throw error;
    }
}
// Then drop the unreadable image blocks from this request, otherwise
// assertImagesFit / prepareFileIds read `versions.get(id).bytes` and crash.
for (const [index, message] of messages.entries()) {
    if (!Array.isArray(message.content)) continue;
    if (!message.content.some((block) => block.type === "image" && !versions.has(block.attachment.attachmentId))) continue;
    const kept = message.content.filter((block) => block.type !== "image" || versions.has(block.attachment.attachmentId));
    messages[index] = { ...message, content: kept.length === 0 ? [{ type: "text", text: "[attachment missing]" }] : kept };
}
dsh-llm-pi-ai — prepareRequestImages / userContent / budget lookup
The same idea, three sites:

prepareRequestImages: replace the Promise.all(...) with per-ref try/catch, keeping only successfully read versions in the map;
userContent (case "image"): when requestImages.get(id) is undefined, push a text placeholder instead of touching version.data;
the requiredImageOffload callback: requestImages.get(id)?.bytes ?? 0.

Notes

Verified with node --check and a real import() of both modules.
Verified end-to-end: after the patch, deleting images that the current session
is actively referencing no longer breaks the conversation.
Also worth considering: marking such attachments as "offloaded" in the session
projection so history replay does not attempt the read again at all.

LLooks like a bug rather than a usage question — can anyone reproduce this on 0.2.1-rc.2?
Symptom
Any MCP tool result containing an image whose data (base64) exceeds ~4.27 MiB characters is replaced by a text placeholder:
[image unavailable: image/gif; Maximum call stack size exceeded; raw image data remains available to programmatic callers]

Source images larger than ~3.2 MB always fail (GIF/PNG/JPEG alike). Not configurable — maxImageBytes defaults to 20 MiB and is never reached.
Root cause
@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client, lib/index.js, decodeImage():
const CANONICAL_BASE64 = /^(?:[A-Za-z0-9+/]{4})*(?:[A-Za-z0-9+/]{2}==|[A-Za-z0-9+/]{3}=)?$/;
...
if (!CANONICAL_BASE64.test(block.data)) throw new Error("the image data is not canonical base64");
.test() on a 4.8 MB string throws RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded (regexp backtracking stack). The caller catches it as a validation error and downgrades the image to text.
Measured on Node 24: 4 MiB passes, 4.27 MiB (4,473,088 chars) throws in ~73 ms.
Version check: 0.2.0-rc.2 and 0.2.1-rc.2 have identical lib/index.js (35041 bytes).
Note: canonicality is already enforced by the very next line if (data.toString("base64") !== block.data) throw ..., so the regex is redundant.
Suggested fix
Drop the regex, or use a linear check:
const CANONICAL_BASE64 = { test(s) {
  if (typeof s !== "string") return false;
  if (s.length === 0) return true;
  if (s.length % 4 !== 0) return false;
  const pad = s.endsWith("==") ? 2 : s.endsWith("=") ? 1 : 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < s.length - pad; i++) {
    const c = s.charCodeAt(i);
    if (!((c >= 48 && c <= 57) || (c >= 65 && c <= 90) || (c >= 97 && c <= 122) || c === 43 || c === 47)) return false;
  }
  return true;
}};
Repro

Expose an MCP tool returning { type: "image", mimeType: "image/gif", data: "<4.8 MB base64>" }.
Call it from DSH on 0.2.1-rc.2.
The tool result is the placeholder text above.

Question / contract gap
I reproduced a synthetic case on @deepseek-ai/dsh-session-persistence-jsonl 0.2.0-rc.2 where equal stat revision tokens before and after a historical parent read do not identify the logical snapshot actually read.
This is a question about the public persistence contract and safe client cache/checkpoint use. I am not claiming that a valid concurrent prefix returned by read violates its documented semantics, nor requesting that clients split or ignore opaque revision tokens.
Related discussion and distinction
Related: #8783 already documents historical revision churn and contains a community dependency-scoping proposal. This post asks a different binding question: even a dependency-scoped observation can return to the same value after a related child is added and removed. I have not tested, installed, or claimed an upstream fix based on that community patch.
Minimal public-API sequence
All resources below belong to a unique temporary synthetic test root. No actual conversation, plugin, model invocation or production Profile is needed.

Materialize a v3 parent with a fixed user title and no child.
Observe stat(parent) token A.
Public create + flush a v4 child with origin:'subagent' and parentSession:parent, then close its writer.
First public open(parent,'read') + read() yields logical snapshot B, including subagent/catalog for that child; close the reader.
Remove the closed, task-owned synthetic child directory, after verifying its identity and scope.
Observe stat(parent) again: its token equals A, and the parent's own file SHA-256 is unchanged.
A fresh independent backend Context reads the parent without that catalog entry.

The corpus observation can return A after a related member is created and removed, while an earlier retained read still validly contains B. A caller using only equal stat endpoints could bind B to an A checkpoint even though a later fresh A read differs.
Verification
Standalone repro.test.mjs uses public SDK services only. Both ordinary JSONL and Zstandard cases passed, as did the cleanup assertion (3 tests). The code is supplied separately in this local feedback bundle; it does not import application-specific plugins or include real Session identifiers, local account paths, log text or credentials.
Environment: Node 25.2.1, macOS arm64, Cordis 4.0.4, DSH SDK packages 0.2.0-rc.2. Backend program SHA-256: 0845707017acc2b4a8a75eab2244fa3fd88587b8094ab32014321dce7ca1e31b.
Fresh dependency installation from the registry was not part of this test. I reused the installed SDK entries with an explicit module-root parameter; that parameter is not a Session storage root.
Requested clarification / possible public capability

Is stat.revision intended solely for lightweight observations, rather than binding a logical body returned by an independently opened reader?
What is the supported way to associate a complete header + events + inheritedEventCount snapshot with a backend-recognized revision for client-side checkpoints?
For historical migration, how should this witness cover related child membership/descriptor facts and A→B→A membership changes?
Is a body-bound snapshot identity, monotonic dependency generation, or supported validation witness available/planned?
What concurrency guarantee should clients assume between snapshot validation and committing a checkpoint to a separate repository? A standalone final stat is not an atomic cross-service commit.

These are capability requirements, not proposed names of APIs that already exist. A plugin-only prototype can own immutable full snapshots and perform bounded rereads, but currently keeps checkpoint admission disabled rather than inventing a binding token or declaring all sources fully indexed.
No SDK code, production logs or Profiles were modified to obtain this result. All exact synthetic roots were closed, removed, and verified absent.
