What I did
I'm developing a local plugin registered through a profile patch:
- insert:
    - id: live-workbench
      name: 'file:///F:/.../plugin/dsh-live-workbench/dist/plugin.js'
The plugin's apply() dynamically imports a handler module that lives elsewhere in my repo, then registers a prefix route with it:
export const inject = ['webServer'];

export function apply(ctx, config) {
  ctx.effect(() => {
    // 带 query 的动态 import：每次重载都拿到全新模块实例
    import(`${HANDLER_URL}?v=${Date.now()}`).then(({ createWorkbenchHandler }) => {
      disposeRoute = ctx.webServer.register({
        kind: 'prefix',
        path: basePath,
        handler: createWorkbenchHandler({ basePath }),
      });
    });
    return () => disposeRoute();
  });
}
What I expected
Editing the handler module and touching cordis.patch.yml should reload the plugin so the new code takes effect — i.e. the documented behaviour that a patch change hot-reloads without restarting DSH.
What actually happened
apply() runs again (my activation breadcrumb confirms it: a new apply() line is appended on every patch touch), but the module it imports is not re-evaluated. Node's ESM cache is keyed by the resolved URL, so the GUI keeps serving the old handler until I restart DSH entirely.
Workaround
Bust the cache manually on every load, as in the snippet above:
import(`${HANDLER_URL}?v=${Date.now()}`)
It works, but the reload is only "real" if the plugin author knows to do this. A plugin that imports statically gets a silently stale engine after a patch reload.
Questions

Is this intended? If so, it would help to document it right next to the hot-reload behaviour — currently the two facts ("patch changes hot-reload" and "imported modules stay cached") lead to opposite conclusions.
Could the loader invalidate the changed plugin's module subtree (or at least log "reloaded entry, imported modules unchanged")?

