openState === "error" && openError !== null && jsx("div", { className: ..., children: t("chat.loadError", { message: openError.message, code: openError.code }) }),
Two independent things go wrong: the error is not classified as a displayable failure, and the state
machine has no transition out of loading other than success or a remote failure. There is no
timeout and no fallback.
Direct verification

All 89 session files in this profile were decompressed frame-by-frame (session.v4.jsonl.zstd is
a concatenation of independent zstd frames, one per record): 0 decompression failures, 0 JSON
parse failures.
All 89 sessions were validated against the full assertSessionWireEvent contract (the 7-key
envelope whitelist, the 61 known event types, surfaceOp rules, sourceEventSeqs rules, and the
per-type data validators): 89/89 pass, zero violations. So the on-disk log is not malformed —
this is not corrupted data, it is a valid log that the paging/snapshot path cannot handle.
Replaying paginate's exact loop over all 34 sessions in this workspace shows the affected session
is the only one that returns hasMore = false with a page larger than maxMessages:
6fc6e014 evs=1017 cut=0 pageLen=1017 hasMore=false. Every other session has cut > 0,
pageLen between 190 and 1666, and hasMore = true.
Measured opening cost per session (decompress + wire serialize, from the tail): 0.9 ms – 486.7 ms.
The affected session's 1,017 events serialize to 4,122,140 bytes; the largest single event is
102,713 bytes (assistant/message at seq 879), and the single request/header is 88,981 bytes
(97 tool schemas).
Archived copy of the affected session was exported and read back to confirm nothing was lost.

Suggested fixes
Immediate mitigation (user-side, no code change needed): rename the session file out of the
way so the session no longer resolves (rejectNotFound → a proper remote error → the pane shows
Failed to load history instead of hanging).
Client-side (the important one): make doOpen fail closed instead of open.
} catch (error) {
  if (generation !== this.openGeneration || this.events !== events) return;
  this.events = void 0;
  this.openState = "error";
  this.openError = error;   // wrap non-remote errors, or at least do not rethrow
}
If rethrowing is intentional (so the caller can handle it), then the state machine still needs a
terminal state for it — reverting to "cold" plus a failed notification, or setting
openState = "error" with a synthesized code such as local/unexpected. Any of those is better than
an eternal loading.
Paging-side: guarantee that the opening snapshot is bounded. Options, in rough order of
preference:

Treat maxMessages as a hard ceiling on the snapshot, not only on the message count — i.e.
also break when the accumulated page exceeds a byte or record budget.
Make turnWindow degrade: if the loop reaches the start of the log without satisfying
minTurns and the page exceeds maxMessages, cut at the maxMessages boundary instead of
returning everything.
Let minTurns be satisfied by the partial turn at the head of the log (there is exactly one
turn here, so "turns crossed" is 1 and can never reach 2 regardless of how far back we walk).

Also worth a look: the maxMessages counter uses 156 messages to describe a 1,017-event,
3.93 MB snapshot. Even in a multi-turn session, injected runtime-context / system-reminder /
time-context events do not count toward maxMessages, so the cap measures something quite
different from what a reader of the option name expects.
Relevant code locations
All offsets are byte offsets into resources/app.asar of Desktop 0.2.1-rc.3:

paginate — ~19,795,542 (same module as ApiSessionController)
sourceFor — ~19,790,957
opening snapshot construction — ~19,789,066
Session.doOpen — ~19,658,166
Session class — ~19,640,266
HISTORY_PAGE_OPTIONS — ~19,639,594
assertSessionWireEvent — ~19,574,631
remoteErrorOf / isRemoteFailure — ~17,780,668
MESSAGE_TYPES$1 / DEFAULT_MAX_MESSAGES — ~19,864,252
chat.loadingHistory / chat.loadError — ~23,845,300 / ~23,620,761 (zh) / ~23,630,016 (en)

Impact
A session that is merely long and single-turn becomes permanently unopenable through the UI, and
the failure mode presents as "still working" rather than "broken". For affected users the content is
only recoverable by reading the session file directly. In my case this blocked access to a session
I needed for follow-up work, and the misdiagnosis cost was significant — the silent loading state
pointed every diagnostic attempt at data corruption, size, plugins, and the Electron profile
directory before the paging logic was found.
